\pard\f2\b\fs28\fi-1020\li1200\fc0\cf0 Class Description \

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 An NSImage object contains an image that can be composited anywhere without first being drawn in any particular view. It manages the image by:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	Reading image data from the application bundle, from an NSPasteboard, or from an NSData object.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Keeping multiple representations of the same image.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Choosing the representation that's appropriate for a particular data type.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Choosing the representation that's appropriate for any given display device.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Caching the representations it uses by rendering them in off-screen windows.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Optionally retaining the data used to draw the representations, so that they can be reproduced when needed.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Compositing the image from the off-screen cache to where it's needed on-screen.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Reproducing the image for the printer so that it matches what's displayed on-screen, yet is the best representation possible for the printed page.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Automatically using any filtering services installed by the user to convert image data from unsupported formats to supported formats. \

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Defining an Image\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 An image can be created from various types of data:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	Encapsulated PostScript code (EPS)\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Bitmap data in Tag Image File Format (TIFF)\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Bitmap data in Windows Bitmap format (BMP)\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Untagged (raw) bitmap data \

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Other image data supported by an NSImageRep subclass registered with the NSImage€class\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Data that can be filtered to a supported type by a user-installed filter service\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 If data is placed in a file (for example, in an application bundle), the NSImage object can access the data whenever it's needed to create the image. If data is read from an NSData object, the NSImage object may need to store the data itself.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Images can also be defined by the program, in two ways:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	By drawing the image in an off-screen window maintained by the NSImage object. In this case, the NSImage maintains only the cached image.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	By defining a method that can be used to draw the image when needed. This allows the NSImage to delegate responsibility for producing the image to some other object.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Image Representations\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 An NSImage object can keep more than one representation of an image. Multiple representations permit the image to be customized for the display device. For example, different hand-tuned TIFF images can be provided for monochrome and color screens, and an EPS representation or a custom method might be used for printing. All representations are versions of the same image.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 An NSImage returns an NSArray of its representations in response to a 
\b representations
\b0  message. Each representation is a kind of NSImageRep object:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx6740\tx9520\fs28\fi-4840\li6740\fc0\cf0 NSEPSImageRep	An image that can be recreated from EPS data that's either stored by the object or at a known location in the file system.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSBitmapImageRep	An image that can be recreated from bitmap or TIFF data.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSCustomImageRep	An image that can be redrawn by a method defined in the application.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 NSCachedImageRep	An image that has been rendered in an off-screen cache from data or instructions that are no longer available. The image in the cache provides the only data from which the image can be reproduced.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 You can define other NSImageRep subclasses for objects that render images from other types of source data. To make these new subclasses available to an NSImage object, they need to be added to the NSImageRep class registry by invoking the 
\b registerImageRepClass:
\b0  class method. NSImage determines the data types that each subclass can support by invoking its 
\b imageUnfilteredFileTypes
\b0  and 
\b imageUnfilteredPasteboardTypes
\b0  methods.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Choosing Representations\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The NSImage object will choose the representation that best matches the rendering device. By default, the choice is made according to the following set of ordered rules. Each rule is applied in turn until the choice of representation is narrowed to one.\

\fs14 \

\fs2 \

\pard\tx2340\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\fi-380\li2340\fc0\cf0 1.	Choose a color representation for a color device, and a gray-scale representation for a monochrome device.\

\fs16 \

\fs28 2.	Choose a representation with a resolution that matches the resolution of the device, or if no representation matches, choose the one with the highest resolution.\

\fs16 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 By default, any image representation with a resolution that's an integer multiple of the device resolution is considered to match. If more than one representation matches, the NSImage will choose the one that's closest to the device resolution. However, you can force resolution matches to be exact by passing NO to the 
\b setMatchesOnMultipleResolution:
\b0  method.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Rule 2 prefers TIFF and bitmap representations, which have a defined resolution, over EPS representations, which don't. However, you can use the 
\b setUsesEPSOnResolutionMismatch:
\b0  method to have the NSImage choose an EPS representation in case a resolution match isn't possible.\

\fs14 \

\fs2 \

\pard\tx2340\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\fi-380\li2340\fc0\cf0 3.	If all else fails, choose the representation with a specified bits per sample that matches the depth of the device. If no representation matches, choose the one with the highest bits per sample.\

\fs16 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 By passing NO to the 
\b setPrefersColorMatch:
\b0  method, you can have the NSImage try for a resolution match before a color match. This essentially inverts the first and second rules above.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 If these rules fail to narrow the choice to a single representationÐfor example, if the NSImage has two color TIFF representations with the same resolution and depthÐthe one that will be chosen is system dependent.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Caching Representations\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 When first asked to composite the image, the NSImage object chooses the representation that's best for the destination display device, as outlined above. It renders the representation in an off-screen window on the same device, then composites it from this cache to the desired location. Subsequent requests to composite the image use the same cache. Representations aren't cached until they're needed for compositing.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 When printing, the NSImage tries not to use the cached image. Instead, it attempts to render on the printerÐusing the appropriate image data, or a delegated methodÐthe best version of the image that it can. Only as a last resort will it image the cached bitmap.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Image Size\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Before an NSImage can be used, the size of the image must be set, in units of the base coordinate system. If a representation is smaller or larger than the specified size, it can be scaled to fit.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 If the size of the image hasn't already been set when the NSImage is provided with a representation, the size will be set from the data. The bounding box is used to determine the size of an NSEPSImageRep. The TIFF fields ªImageLengthº and ªImageWidthº are used to determine the size of an NSBitmapImageRep.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Coordinate Systems\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Images have the horizontal orientation of the base coordinate system; they can't be rotated or horizontally flipped. When composited, an image maintains this orientation, no matter what coordinate system it's composited to. (The destination coordinate system is used only to determine the location of a composited image, not its size or orientation.) Images can be flipped in the vertical direction by using 
\b setFlipped:
\b0 .\

\fs14 \

\fs28 It's possible to refer to portions of an image when compositing by specifying a rectangle in the image's coordinate system, which is identical to the base coordinate system, except that the origin is at the lower left corner of the image.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Named Images\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 An NSImage object can be identified either by its 
\b id
\b0  or by a name. Assigning an NSImage a name adds it to a table kept by the class object; each name in the database identifies one and only one instance of the class. When you ask for an NSImage object by name (with the 
\b imageNamed:
\b0  method), the class object returns the one from its database, which also includes all the system bitmaps provided by the Application Kit. If there's no object in the database for the specified name, the class object tries to create one by checking for a system bitmap of the same name, checking the name of the application's own image, and then checking for the image in the application's main bundle.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 If a file matches the name, an NSImage is created from the data stored there. You can therefore create NSImage objects simply by including EPS, BMP, or TIFF data for them within the executable file, or in files inside the application's file package.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Image Filtering Services\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 NSImage is designed to automatically take advantage of user-installed filter services for converting unsupported image file types to supported image file types. The class method 
\b imageFileTypes
\b0  returns an array of all file types from which NSImage can create an instance of itself. This list includes all file types supported by registered subclasses of NSImageRep, and those types that can be converted to supported file types through a user-installed filter service.\

\fs14 \

