\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Composites the portion of the image enclosed by the 
\i aRect
\i0  rectangle to the location specified by 
\i aPoint
\i0  in the current coordinate system. 
\i aRect
\i0  must be a valid (non-null) rectangle. The 
\i aPoint
\i0  argument is the same as for 
\b compositeToPoint:operation:
\b0 . 
\i op
\i0  should be one of the compositing operations as defined in 
\b dpsOpenStep.h
\b0 .\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The source rectangle is specified relative to a coordinate system that has its origin at the lower left corner of the image, but is otherwise the same as the base coordinate system. \

\fs14 \

\fs28 This method doesn't check to be sure that the rectangle encloses only portions of the image. Therefore, it can conceivably composite areas that don't properly belong to the image, if the 
\i aRect
\i0  rectangle happens to include them. If this turns out to be a problem, you can prevent it from happening by having the NSImage cache its representations in their own individual windows (with the 
\b setCachedSeparately:
\b0  method). In this case, the window's clipping path will prevent anything but the image from being composited.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Compositing part of an image is as efficient as compositing the whole image, but printing just part of an image is not. When printing, it's necessary to draw the whole image and rely on a clipping path to be sure that only the desired portion appears.\

\fs14 \

